                <pb facs="0839" n="1797" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1797"/>

                <head>No. 27.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Graland</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-10-13"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 13,
                        1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I beg to acknowledge your communication of
                    yesterday’s date, by which I am surprised to learn of the failure of the United
                    States marshal at Sitka to obey the instruction of the President of January 26
                    last, for the immediate release of the British sealers <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Onward, Carolena</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic">Thornton</hi>. I have
                    felt it to be my duty at once to communicate this information to the British
                    minister at this capital.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
